      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Apply the defined CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f </path/to/CR>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>If a CR does not exist, <code>oc apply</code> creates a new CR and might trigger the following warning: <code>Warning: oc apply should be used on resources created by either oc create --save-config or oc apply</code>. In this case you can safely ignore this warning.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Obtain a token from the OAuth server.
      <p>As long as the <code>kubeadmin</code> user has been removed, the <code>oc login</code> command provides instructions on how to access a web page where you can retrieve the token.</p>
      <p>You can also access this page from the web console by navigating to <strong>(?) Help</strong> → <strong>Command Line Tools</strong> → <strong>Copy Login Command</strong>.</p>
      </li>
      <li>
      Log in to the cluster, passing in the token to authenticate, by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc login --token=<token>"} lang="terminal" />
      <Alert variant="note" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Note</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>This identity provider does not support logging in with a username and password.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      </li>
      <li>
      Confirm that the user logged in successfully and that the username displays by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc whoami"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
